    "chunk_id": "2026-05-16-islandlk-543d1f6e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fc6a920633080816429cfabf527c2cf2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1343,
    "chunk_text": "Kerala: Left Falls, Not Out\n\nLate Thursday morning, 10 days after the count, Kerala finally heard the referee\u2019s whistle. Top enablers at Indira Bhawan in New Delhi have finalized the selection of the Chief Minister for Kerala, who will govern for the next five years. AICC General Secretary and the party\u2019s in-charge for Kerala and Lakshadweep, Deepa Dasmunsi, announced that the Congress has chosen V. D. Satheesan, shocking many supporters of K. C. Venugopal. But both have accepted the decision and committed to working together, at least publicly. The episode reflects Kerala\u2019s familiar pattern, where internal competition is settled through central authority and public unity follows factional disputes.\n\nBeneath this stability, the political system is no longer shaped by doctrine but by administration, coalition arithmetic, and tightening fiscal constraints. The Congress-led United Democratic Front (UDF) has returned to power after 10 years in opposition, ending the Communist-led Left Democratic Front (LDF) government. The UDF secured 102 seats in the 140-member Assembly, while the LDF was reduced to 35 seats, and the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) won three, marking a clear reversal of the previous mandate. The communists have fallen, but they are not out. This outcome makes it necessary to revisit Kerala\u2019s Left story."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-16-islandlk-543d1f6e",
    "num": "islandlk-543d1f6e",
    "date_str": "2026-05-16",
    "description": "Kerala: Left Falls, Not Out",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/kerala-left-falls-not-out/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1778945820.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-16-islandlk-543d1f6e-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "829b7e0ad428015e8e8fa3c7053d919e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1741,
    "chunk_text": "Beneath this stability, the political system is no longer shaped by doctrine but by administration, coalition arithmetic, and tightening fiscal constraints. The Congress-led United Democratic Front (UDF) has returned to power after 10 years in opposition, ending the Communist-led Left Democratic Front (LDF) government. The UDF secured 102 seats in the 140-member Assembly, while the LDF was reduced to 35 seats, and the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) won three, marking a clear reversal of the previous mandate. The communists have fallen, but they are not out. This outcome makes it necessary to revisit Kerala\u2019s Left story.\n\nIn contrast, several communist and communist-inspired regimes elsewhere were associated with large-scale violence and state breakdown: the Soviet Union under Stalin, Mao\u2019s China, Cambodia under Pol Pot, North Korea under the Kim regime, and Ethiopia under the Derg all saw severe repression and mass deaths. Eastern Europe also experienced violent Soviet interventions in countries such as Hungary and Czechoslovakia to maintain control. Similar patterns of communist-linked insurgency and conflict also emerged in Nepal, Sri Lanka, and other contexts, resulting in tens of thousands of deaths and disappearances.\n\nKerala followed a different path. But constitutional integration comes with consequences. Once a movement stops functioning as an opposition force and becomes part of governance, it begins to absorb the logic of administration. The CPI(M) in Kerala became a tightly organized electoral machine, but over time that discipline turned into rigidity. Governance replaced transformation, welfare delivery replaced structural change, and political conviction itself shifted from practice to narrative."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-16-islandlk-543d1f6e",
    "num": "islandlk-543d1f6e",
