    "md5": "76e1889265b9a5af4b89ed09bc51e8ce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1644,
    "chunk_text": "This fiscal tension matters because Kerala\u2019s politics is heavily driven by welfare expectations. Every electoral cycle expands commitments without fully resolving the question of long-term sustainability. Political parties, regardless of orientation, operate within this constraint. The CPI(M) expands welfare schemes under Left governance, while the Congress-led UDF offers competing promises of welfare expansion and subsidy re-calibration. Political identity becomes secondary to distributive capacity.\n\nNarayana Guru\u2019s influence matters because it created a social environment in which political extremism found limited structural space. Caste reform movements, educational expansion, and social mobility altered traditional hierarchies that often fuel radical political ruptures elsewhere. Kerala\u2019s communism therefore did not arise in a vacuum of deprivation; it emerged within a society already undergoing deep social reform. That context partly explains why communism in Kerala did not evolve into violent insurgency as it did in other parts of the world. The space for armed revolution was constrained by pre-existing social reform trajectories.\n\nOver time, however, even that moral and social foundation has been absorbed into routine political language. All major parties now invoke equality, welfare, and inclusion, but these terms function as electoral jargon rather than transformative principles. The Congress operates through alliance management and electoral calibration rather than coherent political positioning. The CPI(M) governs through administrative efficiency and welfare continuity rather than mass mobilization."
