    "chunk_text": "The latest volume of magazine has once again done its readers proud. Those who shall get their hands on the copies will gather a wealth of knowledge encompassing aviation, Buddhism, flora and fauna, sociology, demographics, archaeology, public transport et al.\n\nBuddhism and Sri Lanka are inseparably intertwined. The novel  Siddhartha published in 1922 written by Hermann Hesse is a book that is more than worthy of a read. On November 12, 1911, Hesse travelled by train from Colombo to Kandy to visit the Temple of the Tooth. It must be said that Hesse\u2019s relationship with the orient was mostly with India rather than Sri Lanka. He regarded India not so much as a physical destination but as an intellectual and spiritual homeland. His visit to our island was also borne out of that relationship. Hesse may perhaps be the most famous German to write on Ceylon, but there were other notable writers too, such as Geiger and Gueth. To know more, peruse the article written so well by Asoka Mendis De Zoysa. It is a very well researched and well presented piece of work.\n\nUndoubtedly the most pleasing aspect of The Ceylon Journal is the images that one comes across within its pages. The quip bordering on  clich\u00e9 is that \u2018a picture is worth a thousand words\u2019. This is especially true when it comes to The Ceylon Journal. It is not some ordinary publication that are dime a dozen.\n\nThe images included in The Ceylon Journal are almost always diligently handpicked before undergoing a process of vetting. It is little doubt that the readers who have given a feedback have often commented on the vitality that it\u2019s images lend to The publication. Illuminating and delightful, they enhance the reading pleasure of its purchasers."
