    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ce23f567f8253c670d39cd8057674db4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "LunuDehi \u2026 spicing up the music scene\n\nWhen one talks of \u2018lunu-dehi\u2019 what comes to mind is the local appetizer \u2026 to enhance the taste of our meals; but that scene has now changed!\n\nPeople have forgotten about the appetizer and refer to LunuDehi as the band that is spicing up the music scene, not only locally, but abroad, as well.\n\nThey did three consecutive tours, recently, lasting five weeks, taking in the USA and Malaysia (with Bathiya & Santhush), and the UK.\n\nThe Malaysian gig, Maristella OBA dinner dance, was a sold out event and, according to reports coming my way, the crowd was on the floor from the very first song, and they danced the night away \u2026 until it was all over.\n\nTheir next foreign assignment is scheduled to commence in September, to Australia, New Zealand and Singapore (with Bathiya & Santhush)) and Germany, in November.\n\nDushan Jayathilake and Nalin Sam are the co-leaders of LunuDehi and they have certainly taken the band to great heights.\n\nDushan, a well-known keyboardist, first gained major recognition through his work with the legendary Gypsies band, where he performed and directed music, under the guidance of Sunil Perera and Piyal.\n\nHe is also widely respected for his exceptional talent in music arranging and composing.\n\nOver the years, Dushan has contributed musical arrangements and productions for some of Sri Lanka\u2019s most celebrated artistes, including Bathiya & Santhush, Chamara Weerasinghe, Laxman Hilmi, Ajith Perera, Ginger White, and Corrine Almeida, among many others.\n\nHis work has played a significant role in shaping the sound of numerous popular Sri Lankan songs.\n\nAs a composer, Dushan has also created original songs, under his own name, including \u2018Mal Sriya\u2019, \u2018Mathakayan\u2019, and \u2018Oyata Seethalada\u2019, and is also highly recognised for creatively remaking classic songs of the Gypsies, while preserving their original spirit and energy, for newer audiences."
  },
