    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "79841a484e8334702df1690911552ae4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1647,
    "chunk_text": "Fuel crisis, global conflict and market abuse: Why Govt. must control unreasonable construction material prices\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s construction sector showed encouraging signs of recovery in 2025, following the severe economic disruptions of preceding years since the 2019 Easter Sunday incident. Improvements in macroeconomic stability, including easing inflation and a relatively stable exchange rate, contributed to a noticeable reduction in construction costs. Key materials such as steel and fuel became more affordable compared to peak crisis levels, allowing developers and contractors to cautiously re-engage in stalled and new projects. However, this fragile recovery is now under threat.\n\nRecent increases in fuel prices, coupled with the ripple effects of ongoing global conflicts, have begun to drive costs upward once again. Given Sri Lanka\u2019s heavy reliance on imported materials and fuel, such external shocks inevitably influence the cost structure of the construction industry. To a certain extent, price adjustments in response to these factors are both expected and economically justified.\n\nYet, a growing concern within the sector is that these conditions are being used as a pretext for exploitative and unjustified price increases. Rather than reflecting genuine cost pressures alone, some suppliers and intermediaries appear to be engaging in profiteering, inflating prices beyond reasonable margins. This lack of transparency and accountability in pricing mechanisms has created an environment where market distortions can thrive. This has happened even during COVID- 19 and economic crisis periods between years 2020 to 2023."
  },
  {
