    "chunk_size_bytes": 1397,
    "chunk_text": "It was a splendid effort and he drew the admiration of the general public for his honesty and efficiency. But others unfortunately got involved in deal making. All in all MR began to lose his popularity due to the shenanigans of his relatives. There was growing dissent largely because Prime Minister Jayaratne was critically ill and MR did not appoint a successor. All these embers of discontent were fanned energetically by CBK who had returned after a long sojourn abroad.\n\nShe visited me in the Ministry once ostensibly to settle some Attanagalla electorate matter but in reality to discuss current politics. I respectfuly told her that I had no intention of supporting the UNP. Later Bhikkhu Ratana met me in the Parliament lounge and pleaded with me to join a coalition to stop MR on the tracks and prevent a Rajapaksa from entering the Presidential race. He too did not get any satisfaction from me.\n\nWhile enjoying a chit chat in the Parliamentary lobby I was told that CBK and some senior UNPers were zeroing in on Maithripala Sirisena as the possible rebel chief against MR. Sirisena was a popular General Secretary of the party after being promoted by party seniors like DM Jayaratne to contest that post against CBK\u2019s nominee SB Dissanayake. After the successful SB defected to the UNP, he was appointed Secretary of the party and CBK apologized publicly for overlooking him earlier."
