    "chunk_size_bytes": 1707,
    "chunk_text": "However after this meeting Maitri was taken to a discussion with pro MR Buddhist monks parked elsewhere in the building. He was asked to swear with a relic casket on his head that he would not contest the coming presidential election. Being a good politician Maitri did so without batting an eyelid and after receiving the thanks of the departing monks was treated to a hopper dinner by MR.\n\nAfter dinner, and many assurances later, he departed and on the following day announced his candidature at a press conference together with a handful of MP\u2019s who crossed over along with him.\n\nOne of the factors which persuaded Maitri to enter the fray was a statistical study made by a young researcher named Malith Jayatilleke which concluded that MR could be beaten electorally. He had studied the voting patterns of electorates in depth and said that with a proper election campaign Maitri could emerge the winner. Jayatilleke came from a Communist family well known to DEW Gunasekera, the Communist leader. DEW presented Malith\u2019s data to a group, which included me, at his office in the Senior Minister\u2019s building.\n\nHe also made a presentation to MR and Basil and recommended that they should go the full term of the Presidency and not hold a premature election. But this was rejected and a witch hunt was launched against Jayatilleke. He was called a UNP plant even though DEW vouched for him. At that time Jayatilleke was employed as an administrative officer in Dullas Alahapperuma\u2019s Ministry. Dullas was forced to dismiss him. This was another blunder since Jayatilleke joined Maitri\u2019s campaign as a statistical advisor. He was nominated as a National list MP later by the newly elected President."
