    "chunk_size_bytes": 1935,
    "chunk_text": "He also made a presentation to MR and Basil and recommended that they should go the full term of the Presidency and not hold a premature election. But this was rejected and a witch hunt was launched against Jayatilleke. He was called a UNP plant even though DEW vouched for him. At that time Jayatilleke was employed as an administrative officer in Dullas Alahapperuma\u2019s Ministry. Dullas was forced to dismiss him. This was another blunder since Jayatilleke joined Maitri\u2019s campaign as a statistical advisor. He was nominated as a National list MP later by the newly elected President.\n\nAfter protesting that he should be the common candidate Ranil bowed to internal and external pressures and withdrew his candidature in exchange for a promise that he would be made Prime Minister. There was no doubt that the UNP would be the main contributor to the Sirisena campaign. As the under dog he gathered UNP votes, as required by Malith\u2019s statistical analysis, by flattering Ranil and threatening hell and brimstone for the Rajapaksas and their cohorts.\n\nI got back to Kandy and found that MR\u2019s cousin Udayanga Weeratunga had been put to oversee the campaign there. I had no difficulty working with him as I knew him well and also because he was a congenial person. Soon he must have reported that there was no mischief afoot in Kandy and he was transferred to some other district. It was a close race in Kandy but Sirisena swept the board. However we did well in Galagedera electorate which was won by MR.\n\nSaman Kelegama analysed the unexpected results of the 2015 election in the following manner; \u201cAlthough the margin of the incoming President Maithripala Sirisena\u2019s victory was thin, he managed to make substantial inroads in the majority Sinhala Buddhist vote base [70 percent of the population] of Rajapaksa. This, capped by overwhelming support from the minority communities in Sri Lanka, enabled Sirisena to romp home."
