    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "The Majority\u2019s Moon\n\nSharni Jayawardena\u2019s film \u2018Just a Phase\u2019 \u2013 on the Holidays Act, No. 17 of 1965 \u2013 treats the forgotten statute as what it always was: a quarrel, waged through politics, nationalism, and religiosity, over who gets to define the nation\u2019s sense of time, and on what basis. The Act did two stark things. It stripped Sunday of its inherited legal status, and made all four Poya Days each month (not just the full moon day) both a public and bank holiday \u2013 a regime that came into force on January 1, 1966. Beyond administrative tidying, this was the state reordering public life around a vaulting Sinhala-Buddhist nationalism\u2019s underlying faith-based rhythm, which the film grasps from its first frames.\n\nThe craft on display is considerable, though anyone who has followed Jayawardena\u2019s earlier work would expect no less. The photography, and location work move between intimate interiors, and the framing of human subjects to more open landscapes that hold the country\u2019s uneasy inheritance in a single frame. The archival research is patient, multidisciplinary, and beautifully presented: newspaper clippings in Sinhala, Tamil and English, ephemera like the Elephant Beedi calendar, legal gazettes, parliamentary debates and oral testimony stitched together with a care that speaks to months, perhaps years, of work.\n\nThe film surfaces Dharmapala\u2019s 1898 daily code advising women to treat sil not as burden but as regular practice, which is a small detail saying a great deal about the gendered moral architecture the Act was quietly importing. Sound mixing rarely gets the credit it deserves in documentary, and the immersive mix here is exceptional \u2013 I watched with headphones, which certainly helped. The background music, and sound effects, field recordings, archival clips, and the voices of those who lived through the period sit in a balance that rarely draws attention to itself. That restraint is the craft."
