    "md5": "ab8a77a54a03fbb0382f3c2172d7dcf8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1521,
    "chunk_text": "The film surfaces Dharmapala\u2019s 1898 daily code advising women to treat sil not as burden but as regular practice, which is a small detail saying a great deal about the gendered moral architecture the Act was quietly importing. Sound mixing rarely gets the credit it deserves in documentary, and the immersive mix here is exceptional \u2013 I watched with headphones, which certainly helped. The background music, and sound effects, field recordings, archival clips, and the voices of those who lived through the period sit in a balance that rarely draws attention to itself. That restraint is the craft.\n\nOne insight the film surfaces, and which deserves more weight than it gets, is that the popular practice of Buddhism during this period carried on largely independent of its political instrumentalisation. The lunar calendar was the creation of populist politicians, and a narrow clerical establishment pressing electoral advantage towards majoritarian rule. Sil, and everyday observance belonged to a different register, one that did not need a statute to function. This gap between lived faith, and politicised (or weaponised) faith is the quiet centre of the film, and where it most clearly speaks to a policymaking pathology that still bedevils the country, and how! The primacy of Buddhism in the 1972 constitution, the majoritarian reflexes that shape public holidays, language policy, everyday exchanges, and electoral rhetoric alike belongs to the trajectory the 1965 Act set, and its repeal did not fully end."
