    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "One insight the film surfaces, and which deserves more weight than it gets, is that the popular practice of Buddhism during this period carried on largely independent of its political instrumentalisation. The lunar calendar was the creation of populist politicians, and a narrow clerical establishment pressing electoral advantage towards majoritarian rule. Sil, and everyday observance belonged to a different register, one that did not need a statute to function. This gap between lived faith, and politicised (or weaponised) faith is the quiet centre of the film, and where it most clearly speaks to a policymaking pathology that still bedevils the country, and how! The primacy of Buddhism in the 1972 constitution, the majoritarian reflexes that shape public holidays, language policy, everyday exchanges, and electoral rhetoric alike belongs to the trajectory the 1965 Act set, and its repeal did not fully end.\n\nThere are surely so many others. What do younger Sri Lankans, encountering this history now for the first time, make of a state that once tried to legislate the weekend, and by extension, the work week, around the moon\u2019s phases? That conversation would have pulled the past into the present, where it belongs.\n\nThe Act was essentially statecraft informed by, and seeking to influence labour, ritual and an electoral arithmetic. The film is strongest when it remembers this, as it does when it returns to the 1971 repeal. The Holidays Act, No. 29 passed that year retained only the Full Moon Poya Day alongside Sunday, and the muted public response suggests little popular attachment to the earlier arrangement. What stays after watching \u2018Just a Phase\u2019 is the film\u2019s account of the damage done when a state mistakes symbolic majoritarianism for shared national meaning, and social cohesion."
