    "md5": "49a1bc25b8aad24b32009c4428f9eed7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1357,
    "chunk_text": "End of Peacekeeping\n\nWe are facing a problem that persists despite endless rhetoric and an accelerating collapse of actionable plans. A new report from the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute (SIPRI), scheduled to be launched tomorrow, 25 May, assesses that personnel deployments to peace operations have declined sharply to 78,633\u2014just over half the number deployed in 2016 and the lowest level in at least 25 years. That figure is not just a statistical dip in an otherwise stable system; it is a measurable contraction of political will, institutional reach and, ultimately, belief in the idea that collective security can still be administered through multilateral peacekeeping at all.\n\nWhat is being described here is not a temporary funding shortfall or an operational adjustment cycle. It is a structural withdrawal. Peace operations are not collapsing in the dramatic fashion of failed states or defeated armies. They are being slowly starved, re-scoped and politically marginalized, while the language of commitment remains intact. Ironically, governments still endorse peacekeeping in principle, still send delegates to conferences, still repeat familiar pledges, yet the material foundation required to sustain those pledges is being withdrawn year after year. The result is a system that performs continuity while losing capacity."
  },
