    "md5": "3ad1a1b67e7ffba3e76d0da87f667a5d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1334,
    "chunk_text": "What is being described here is not a temporary funding shortfall or an operational adjustment cycle. It is a structural withdrawal. Peace operations are not collapsing in the dramatic fashion of failed states or defeated armies. They are being slowly starved, re-scoped and politically marginalized, while the language of commitment remains intact. Ironically, governments still endorse peacekeeping in principle, still send delegates to conferences, still repeat familiar pledges, yet the material foundation required to sustain those pledges is being withdrawn year after year. The result is a system that performs continuity while losing capacity.\n\nThere is a temptation in policy circles to treat this as cyclical decline, something that can be corrected with renewed political attention or incremental reform. That reading is increasingly implausible. The more accurate interpretation is that peacekeeping has entered a phase of geopolitical incompatibility. The Security Council, rather than acting as a stabilizing mechanism, now reflects the fragmentation it is meant to manage. Veto politics, strategic rivalry and transactional diplomacy have replaced the post-Cold War assumption that consensus on minimum security provision was possible. Even routine mandate renewals have become arenas of obstruction and conditionality."
