    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "There is a temptation in policy circles to treat this as cyclical decline, something that can be corrected with renewed political attention or incremental reform. That reading is increasingly implausible. The more accurate interpretation is that peacekeeping has entered a phase of geopolitical incompatibility. The Security Council, rather than acting as a stabilizing mechanism, now reflects the fragmentation it is meant to manage. Veto politics, strategic rivalry and transactional diplomacy have replaced the post-Cold War assumption that consensus on minimum security provision was possible. Even routine mandate renewals have become arenas of obstruction and conditionality.\n\nWhat replaces multilateral peacekeeping in this vacuum is not neutrality or local self-correction, but fragmentation. The SIPRI analysis points to the proliferation of ad hoc coalitions, bilateral deployments and regionally constrained missions. These arrangements are not inherently illegitimate, but they are structurally different from UN peacekeeping in one decisive respect: they are driven by national interest rather than collective mandate. Rwanda, the United Arab Emirates, Uganda and others now deploy forces abroad under bilateral agreements that reflect strategic alignment rather than universal norms.\n\nMartin Bell, reflecting on earlier cycles of intervention, warned in a different context that war produces only losers and that peacekeeping offers outcomes rather than victories. That distinction now feels almost understated. The more pressing issue is that even \u201coutcomes\u201d are becoming increasingly difficult to produce. When peace operations are reduced in scale, restricted in mandate and politically constrained in the use of force, they cease to function as instruments of conflict management and become symbolic presences in environments where coercive actors face little meaningful constraint. The language of protection persists, but the capacity to enforce protection diminishes."
