    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "Martin Bell, reflecting on earlier cycles of intervention, warned in a different context that war produces only losers and that peacekeeping offers outcomes rather than victories. That distinction now feels almost understated. The more pressing issue is that even \u201coutcomes\u201d are becoming increasingly difficult to produce. When peace operations are reduced in scale, restricted in mandate and politically constrained in the use of force, they cease to function as instruments of conflict management and become symbolic presences in environments where coercive actors face little meaningful constraint. The language of protection persists, but the capacity to enforce protection diminishes.\n\nThe UN\u2019s experience in recent missions illustrates this erosion. Mandates have become more complex while political backing has grown more fragile. Forces are deployed with expectations of civilian protection but without the political consensus required to sustain robust engagement. The result is a structural contradiction: missions are authorized to stabilize environments they are not adequately resourced or politically empowered to influence. This is not a failure of personnel. It is a failure of design under contemporary geopolitical conditions.\n\nThe most consequential shift, however, is normative. The idea that multilateral peacekeeping represents a shared international responsibility is weakening. States increasingly treat engagement in peace operations as optional, contingent on domestic political calculation rather than international obligation. This is reflected not only in funding decisions but also in the gradual narrowing of acceptable mandate language, particularly around issues such as human rights monitoring, climate impacts and gender inclusion."
