    "chunk_text": "The most consequential shift, however, is normative. The idea that multilateral peacekeeping represents a shared international responsibility is weakening. States increasingly treat engagement in peace operations as optional, contingent on domestic political calculation rather than international obligation. This is reflected not only in funding decisions but also in the gradual narrowing of acceptable mandate language, particularly around issues such as human rights monitoring, climate impacts and gender inclusion.\n\nWhat is often left unspoken is that peacekeeping depends not only on money and mandates but on a shared belief in the legitimacy of external stabilization. That belief is eroding. The result is a quiet reversion to a more transactional international order in which security is managed through selective partnerships, bilateral alignments and episodic interventions. It is not an absence of order, but a different kind of order\u2014one that is less transparent, less accountable and more unevenly distributed. Consequently, what is ending is not the existence of missions but the coherence of the system that once made them part of a unified framework. What remains is a patchwork of interventions that may still prevent collapse in specific locations but no longer constitute a global model of conflict management.\n\nKofi Annan, in his last interview with the BBC in Geneva in April 2018, sounded the alarm about the worldwide rise of autocracies while still expressing the hope that the pendulum would swing back. \u201cThere are so many issues,\u201d he said, \u201cthat no one country can resolve.\u201d He called himself a stubborn optimist, and it was not a casual description. He had confronted military dictatorships, especially in his native Africa, with a seriousness of purpose few of his successors matched, and he insisted that there could be no security without development, no development without security, and neither security nor development without human rights."
