    "chunk_text": "TOXIC WATERS: How Galle\u2019s rivers are becoming invisible hotspots for superbugs and disease risk\n\nGALLE\u2013 A pioneering scientific study conducted by an international team of Sri Lankan and Japanese researchers has delivered a wake-up call regarding the deteriorating health of urban rivers in the historic coastal city of Galle. The investigation reveals a hazardous cocktail of untreated urban wastewater and seawater intrusion that is quietly transforming local waterways into breeding grounds for antibiotic-resistant \u201csuperbugs\u201d and posing serious health risks to river using communities.\n\nThe study, which meticulously analysed three key rivers\u2014Gin Ganga, Kepu Ela, and Moragoda Ela\u2014is the first of its kind in Sri Lanka to integrate advanced DNA sequencing, antibiotic resistance tracking, and public health risk modeling.\n\nThe research established a direct, undeniable link between urban congestion and water degradation. Among the three waterways, Moragoda Ela emerged as a critical pollution hotspot. With up to 89% of its surrounding catchment area heavily urbanised and packed with residential buildings, the river has essentially been forced to absorb massive volumes of untreated domestic and hospital wastewater.\n\nTesting revealed that Moragoda Ela contains alarmingly high levels of E. coli (fecal bacteria) and elevated Chemical Oxygen Demand (COD), an indicator of heavy chemical and organic pollution. In stark contrast, the larger Gin Ganga and the less urbanised Kepu Ela maintained significantly lower levels of contamination, proving that dense urban footprints are actively overwhelming our smaller coastal streams.\n\nThe Illusion of Cleanliness: The \u201cInvisible\u201d Danger of Seawater Intrusion"
