    "chunk_text": "Testing revealed that Moragoda Ela contains alarmingly high levels of E. coli (fecal bacteria) and elevated Chemical Oxygen Demand (COD), an indicator of heavy chemical and organic pollution. In stark contrast, the larger Gin Ganga and the less urbanised Kepu Ela maintained significantly lower levels of contamination, proving that dense urban footprints are actively overwhelming our smaller coastal streams.\n\nThe Illusion of Cleanliness: The \u201cInvisible\u201d Danger of Seawater Intrusion\n\nTo the untrained eye, traditional water tests at this location might suggest the water is cleaning itself, as the number of culturable, live  E. coli bacteria drop sharply. However, the researchers discovered this is a dangerous illusion.\n\n\u201cThe high salt levels don\u2019t necessarily kill the bacteria,\u201d explained one of the leading authors. \u201cInstead, the stress forces the E. coli into a \u2018sleep mode\u2019 known as a Viable But Non-Culturable (VBNC) state. They become invisible to standard laboratory culture tests, yet they survive, retain their dangerous properties, and continue to harbor hazardous genes.\u201d\n\nFurthermore, using sophisticated 16S rRNA DNA sequencing, the team found that while standard E. coli seemed to vanish, other resilient fecal bacteria like Bacteroidales thrived in the salty, brackish water. This discovery means our current national methods for testing coastal water quality may be dangerously outdated, failing to detect active fecal pollution in brackish environments. (See Image 2)\n\nPerhaps the most alarming finding for the general public is the widespread distribution of Antibiotic Resistance Genes (ARGs). These genes allow ordinary bacteria to mutate into superbugs that can resist modern life-saving medications.\n\nThe team tracked two specific resistance genes: mphA (which resists common macrolide antibiotics like azithromycin) and blaIMP (which resists critical, last-resort hospital antibiotics)."
