    "chunk_text": "Perhaps the most alarming finding for the general public is the widespread distribution of Antibiotic Resistance Genes (ARGs). These genes allow ordinary bacteria to mutate into superbugs that can resist modern life-saving medications.\n\nThe team tracked two specific resistance genes: mphA (which resists common macrolide antibiotics like azithromycin) and blaIMP (which resists critical, last-resort hospital antibiotics).\n\nwas found distributed across all three rivers, regardless of whether the area was urban or rural. This indicates that dangerous hospital-grade antibiotic resistance has already escaped containment and is structurally embedded across Galle\u2019s natural river networks.\n\nBecause river water in these areas is widely diverted to sustain livelihoods probably. Hence, the study used a global standard modeling technique called Quantitative Microbial Risk Assessment (QMRA) to determine the exact danger to human health.\n\nThe analysis calculated the health risk in \u201cDisability-Adjusted Life Years\u201d (DALYs), which measures the years of healthy life lost due to illness.\n\nThe World Health Organization (WHO) sets a strict safety threshold of 10-6 DALY (one-in-a-million risk level) per year.\n\nThe mathematical simulations revealed that Moragoda Ela failed the safety test completely, with a 100% exceedance rate of the WHO safety guideline. Users (maybe for irrigational activities) incidentally intact may face an unacceptably high annual risk of contracting severe diarrheal and enteric illnesses. Fortunately, the Gin Ganga and Kepu Ela rivers currently remain within the safer limits. (I1)\n\nWith less than 10% of Sri Lanka\u2019s land mass supported by centralised wastewater treatment systems, the researchers stress that we can no longer treat our rivers as open sewers.\n\nAs a benchmark for a solution, the study compared Galle\u2019s rivers against highly purified \u201creclaimed water\u201d from a tertiary wastewater treatment facility in Sapporo, Japan."
