    "language": "en",
    "md5": "02656e187b1312dedcf279ec08e0d457",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1416,
    "chunk_text": "With less than 10% of Sri Lanka\u2019s land mass supported by centralised wastewater treatment systems, the researchers stress that we can no longer treat our rivers as open sewers.\n\nAs a benchmark for a solution, the study compared Galle\u2019s rivers against highly purified \u201creclaimed water\u201d from a tertiary wastewater treatment facility in Sapporo, Japan.\n\nThe risk of illness from this reclaimed water was virtually zero, maintaining the highest possible margin of safety.\n\nThe international research coalition is urging Sri Lankan authorities to act immediately by implementing three crucial interventions:\n\n=Upgrade Urban Infrastructure: Establish localized, decentralised wastewater treatment plants in dense urban pockets, especially near major public institutions like hospitals.\n\n=Modernize Water Monitoring: Move away from relying solely on basic E. coli cultures in coastal estuaries. Authorities must adopt salinity-resilient microbial markers (such as Bacteroidales) to get an honest picture of water safety.\n\n=Invest in Water Reclamation: Begin exploring modern wastewater recycling systems to produce safe, treated water dedicated to agricultural irrigation, preserving our pristine freshwater sources and protecting the health of our farmers.\n\nIf we fail to act, the pristine coastal image of Galle will remain a mask hiding a brewing ecological and public health emergency right beneath the surface."
