    "chunk_size_bytes": 1656,
    "chunk_text": "May Day can mean one of many things. In European countries it is a festival, dating back nearly a thousand years, celebrating the beginning of the summer. People across the UK, especially in villages, hold fetes on 1st of May, celebrating May Day in different ways including maypole dancing, Morris dancing and the crowning of a May Queen. In many other countries it is a celebration of the International Labour Day, its origin dating back to the 1886 Haymarket Affair in Chicago where workers striking for an eight-hour day faced violent suppression, resulting in many deaths and the execution of some labour leaders. Sri Lanka is among around 80 countries celebrating this event of worker\u2019s solidarity though it has become highly politicised, unfortunately.\n\nOn the other hand, \u2018mayday\u2019 repeated thrice, is the universal call sign for distress used by aviators and sailors, other emergency services too adopting this in some countries.\n\nWhat transpired at the government May Day rallies raises the spectre of a mayday, no doubt!\u00a0 However, what is not sure is whether the danger is to the government or to the electoral process. Perhaps, it is for both, as a failing government may look for alternatives to democracy. Wonder whether the words of Tilvin Silva, the power behind the throne, would turn out to be prophetic. Addressing one of the many NPP May Day rallies, he proclaimed to the applause of his supporters that there would be no future governments. After a pause he added that the opposition does not know how to form a government. Whether he did so to soften the blow or on the realisation that he had dropped a clanger, we do not know!"
