    "md5": "0ad30c59d67c3150c7c3eb7fa73c8f19",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1267,
    "chunk_text": "There was excessive private credit growth in the first quarter of this year. It exceeds an annualized figure of over 20%. The IMF wanted to have private credit growth below 10% for the years 2025, 2026 and 2027, unadjusted for inflation. The private credit or loans, created by commercial banks, are virtual money, sometimes known as \u201ccredit money.\u201d Therefore, any increase in private credit is like printing new money. This is an important monetary parameter. That\u2019s the very reason the IMF wanted the private credit growth to be in the upper single-digit range. The government lifted the ban on passenger car imports, but took no precautions to contain private credit growth as agreed with the IMF.\n\nNow, some suggest to shrink the economy. This is not a proper solution. Instead, the solution is to contain the private credit growth so that the economy will grow in a reduced phase while stabilizing the currency in the short term.\n\nStable exchange rate mechanism means the predictability of the rupee, subject to a stable depreciation of 5 to 7 per cent annually. There is no need to bring it back to 300 for a USD range. Ultimately, there are chances that the IMF might pressure the government to stick to the cost recovery prices for fuel and electricity."
