    "md5": "35a283fbef5023ef9fc39c154b78b0ea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1576,
    "chunk_text": "SL\u2019s apparel exports record softer drop in April as annual projection falls below US$ 5bn\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s apparel sector witnessed a softening in its April performance, with total exports declining by a softer 4.72 percent to US$ 328.15 million, compared to US$ 344.40 million in the corresponding month of 2025.\n\nCumulatively for the first four months of 2026, export earnings contracted by 7.47 percent to US$ 1.53 billion, reflecting continued pressure across major export destinations. With these persisting challenges, the industry\u2019s annual earnings are projected to fall short of the crucial US$ 5 billion milestone.\n\nSpeaking to Mirror Business, Joint Apparel Association Forum (JAAF) Chairman Felix Fernando confirmed the adjusted outlook, noting, \u201cIt will be a little less than $5 billion, around 4.8 to 4.9 billion.\u201d\n\nThe contraction is largely a reflection of suppressed demand in traditional strongholds, as widespread economic pressures dampen consumer spending. This suppressed demand is starkly visible in the performance of major global apparel brands that source heavily from Sri Lanka, including Victoria\u2019s Secret, Nike, Tommy Hilfiger, Calvin Klein, and Marks & Spencer. These industry giants are currently navigating a cautious retail environment characterized by tightened inventory and softer consumer appetites. For instance, recent financial metrics from leading sportswear brands like Nike indicate expected revenue contractions of around 5 percent and squeezed margins in early 2026, as digital sales and store traffic face global headwinds."
