    "chunk_text": "The contraction is largely a reflection of suppressed demand in traditional strongholds, as widespread economic pressures dampen consumer spending. This suppressed demand is starkly visible in the performance of major global apparel brands that source heavily from Sri Lanka, including Victoria\u2019s Secret, Nike, Tommy Hilfiger, Calvin Klein, and Marks & Spencer. These industry giants are currently navigating a cautious retail environment characterized by tightened inventory and softer consumer appetites. For instance, recent financial metrics from leading sportswear brands like Nike indicate expected revenue contractions of around 5 percent and squeezed margins in early 2026, as digital sales and store traffic face global headwinds.\n\nIn Europe, the challenge of lowered demand is exacerbated by fierce regional competition from Vietnam and Bangladesh. \u201cIn Europe, people can\u2019t understand this, but there is a lot of import coming from Vietnam and Bangladesh,\u201d Fernando stated, highlighting a critical structural disadvantage regarding trade concessions. \u201cLess than 60 percent (of Sri Lankan apparel) qualified for GSP Plus due to fabric origin, which doesn\u2019t apply for Bangladesh.\u201d Despite the overarching decline, isolated pockets of resilience remain, with local manufacturers like Omegaline recording a marginal uptick. Meanwhile, potential geopolitical shifts in the U.S. could inadvertently offer a silver lining. Addressing the prospect of new trade policies, Fernando suggested that a \u201cTrump tariff could put Sri Lanka on par with Vietnam if it levels it up.\u201d\n\nTo mitigate the volatility of traditional destinations, JAAF is heavily emphasizing market diversification to support the sector. While core markets stumbled, exports to other markets recorded a 12.61 percent growth during April, signaling that there is still room to build momentum through greater exploration. \u201cWe should be able to do more in the UK and European developing countries,\u201d Fernando emphasised. (NF)"
