    "chunk_text": "TPA\u2019s Barath questions JVP\u2019s \u2018Indian Expansion\u2019 narrative\n\nMay 25 (Daily Mirror) - A rare and politically significant acknowledgement on the historical injustices faced by the Indian Origin Malayaga Tamil community was made in Parliament last week during a special adjournment motion brought by Deputy Leaders of the Tamil Progressive Alliance (TPA), MPs V. Radhakrishnan and Palani Thigambaram.\n\nSpeaking during the debate, Minister Bimal Rathnayake openly acknowledged that political decisions, discriminatory state policies and historical political narratives contributed to the suffering of the IOT Malayaha people after independence.\n\nThe Vice President of the DPF and the politburo member of the Tamil Progressive Alliance figure Barath Arullsamy described the Minister\u2019s speech as \u201cone of the most honest acknowledgements ever made inside Parliament by a Sinhala mainstream political leader regarding the historical treatment of the Indian Origin Tamil community.\u201d\n\n\u201cFor decades, our community carried a painful historical burden that many refused to openly acknowledge,\u201d Arullsamy stated.\n\nHe noted that before 1948, all communities in Ceylon were recognised as British subjects. However, after independence, the very people who helped build Sri Lanka\u2019s plantation economy and infrastructure became stateless in the very land they helped develop.\n\n\u201cOur people cleared jungles, built roads, railways, plantations and contributed immensely to the national economy. Yet citizenship itself became a struggle that continued until 2003,\u201d he said.\n\nArullsamy further highlighted that Minister Rathnayake had made a striking observation in Parliament by pointing out that Tamil communities taken by the British to countries such as Malaysia, Mauritius and Fiji had progressed socially and economically, while those brought to Sri Lanka endured generations of statelessness, disenfranchisement, exclusion and political suppression."
  },
