    "md5": "d36657720e964c0c21f7b7b744c59930",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "Arullsamy further highlighted that Minister Rathnayake had made a striking observation in Parliament by pointing out that Tamil communities taken by the British to countries such as Malaysia, Mauritius and Fiji had progressed socially and economically, while those brought to Sri Lanka endured generations of statelessness, disenfranchisement, exclusion and political suppression.\n\nHowever, Arullsamy noted, somewhat ironically, that while the Minister courageously acknowledged many uncomfortable truths, he stopped just short of addressing another painful chapter: how generations of Malayaga Tamils were politically vilified under the banner of resisting so-called \u201cIndian expansionism.\u201d\n\n\u201cFor decades, a poor plantation worker carrying nothing more dangerous than a tea basket was politically portrayed as part of an Indian expansion project,\u201d Arullsamy remarked.\n\nHe stated that extremist political propaganda during past decades, particularly narratives aggressively amplified during earlier political periods by the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna, helped cultivate suspicion and hostility toward the Indian Origin Tamil community.\n\n\u201cWhile our people were struggling for citizenship, land, education and survival, they were simultaneously treated as a demographic threat. That contradiction is one of the great political ironies of post-independence Sri Lanka,\u201d he added.\n\nArullsamy nevertheless praised Minister Rathnayake and the present government for allowing such difficult discussions to emerge openly in Parliament, noting that many political leaders in the past lacked the courage to publicly address these realities.\n\n\u201cI have great respect for Minister Bimal Rathnayake for honestly bringing these issues into the national political conversation inside Parliament. Acknowledgement is the beginning of reconciliation,\u201d he said."
