    "chunk_size_bytes": 2028,
    "chunk_text": "Defying Power Politics: Chinese Ambassador hails the  \u2018Rubber-Rice\u2019 spirit\n\nAmbassador Qi Zhenhong unveils China\u2019s comprehensive stabilization framework for Sri Lanka, spanning deep humanitarian pledges, alternative green energy pipelines, and shared Global South Alliances\n\n\u201cMore than 70 years ago, breaking through immense obstacles, our two nations signed the historic Rubber-Rice Pact. This agreement demonstrated the unyielding spirit and fortitude of both peoples in defying power politics.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe root cause of the conflict is the US-Israeli launch of military attacks against Iran in violation of international law. This is a war that should not have happened\u2014it is a war that does no one any good.\u201d\n\n\u201cChina has supplied 70% of the world\u2019s wind power equipment and 80% of its photovoltaic modules, driving down global wind power and solar power costs by more than 60% and 80%, respectively.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe world lacks order not because the UN is outdated, but exactly because the purposes and principles of the UN Charter are being breached and distorted.\u201d\n\nIn a comprehensive diplomatic review, Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Qi Zhenhong has re-anchored the bilateral relationship within a two-thousand-year historical legacy, tracing ties from the ancient Rubber-Rice Pact to modern crisis interventions. Addressing critical geopolitical shifts, including the US-Iran conflict and a mutating global energy market, the Ambassador detailed Beijing\u2019s multi-million dollar humanitarian deployment following Cyclone Ditwah, its massive clean-energy infrastructure rollouts, and a firm defensive stance on reforming the United Nations. Empathizing with Sri Lanka\u2019s economic vulnerability, the envoy outlined a strategic path forward centered on \u201cSmall yet Smart\u201d livelihood projects and next-generation green cooperation.\n\nQ China and Sri Lanka has very long friendship which was renewed in 1970\u2019s by Mrs Srimavo Bandaranaike in the 70s. What do you say about the long history of our friendship?"
