    "md5": "c81025d1ab5b134a6f469fbb37029820",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1690,
    "chunk_text": "Ceylon Cold Stores absorbs inflationary shocks and climate disruptions to protect market share\n\nCeylon Cold Stores PLC, the operator of the Elephant House brand, has deliberately absorbed significant inflationary pressures and extreme weather-related disruptions during the financial year 2025/26, sacrificing short-term margin expansions to aggressively defend its consumer market share.\n\nNavigating a severely tested operational landscape, the fast-moving consumer goods giant opted against fully passing on escalating input costs to heavily burdened consumers, maintaining volume momentum across its core beverage and confectionery portfolios despite a deeply volatile macroeconomic environment. This calculated margin compression strategy reflects a broader corporate objective to prioritize long-term brand equity and customer retention over immediate profitability in a highly price-sensitive market.\n\nThe domestic manufacturing sector faced a barrage of compounding headwinds throughout the year, driven by a sharp escalation in global commodity prices, particularly across cocoa, dairy, and nut supply chains.\n\nThis global price volatility was further exacerbated locally by sweeping domestic electricity tariff hikes and persistent exchange rate fluctuations, which heavily inflated the cost of production. Compounding these structural economic challenges were acute physical climate risks, most notably the severe impact of Cyclone Ditwah. The extreme weather event triggered substantial logistical and distribution bottlenecks while severely dampening out-of-home consumption patterns during what is traditionally the peak seasonal quarter for the beverage and ice cream sectors."
  },
