    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d679022923f576a250686ed009523a73",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "This global price volatility was further exacerbated locally by sweeping domestic electricity tariff hikes and persistent exchange rate fluctuations, which heavily inflated the cost of production. Compounding these structural economic challenges were acute physical climate risks, most notably the severe impact of Cyclone Ditwah. The extreme weather event triggered substantial logistical and distribution bottlenecks while severely dampening out-of-home consumption patterns during what is traditionally the peak seasonal quarter for the beverage and ice cream sectors.\n\nElaborating on this strategic positioning in his review within the Ceylon Cold Stores PLC Annual Report 2025/26, Chairperson Krishan Balendra stated, \u201cMargins remained under pressure during the year due to elevated global prices of key raw materials such as cocoa, dairy inputs, and nuts, alongside higher electricity tariffs and fuel costs where the category took a conscious decision to absorb the majority of these price pressures without passing them on to the consumers through retail price increase.\u201d\n\nTo support this sustained volume growth and prepare for future demand cycles, the entity simultaneously committed substantial capital injections into its manufacturing capabilities, seamlessly deploying Rs. 4.78 billion in strategic capacity expansion investments during the period. These targeted infrastructural upgrades are designed to enhance production efficiencies and mitigate future supply chain vulnerabilities, complementing the company\u2019s broader operational resilience. By shielding its core demographic from the worst of the economic and environmental shocks while quietly reinforcing its production infrastructure, Ceylon Cold Stores effectively solidified its foundational market dominance as the domestic consumer economy transitions toward a phase of gradual stabilization."
  },
