    "md5": "dea657d14796daa17ee6a0e51b5bb6ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "As a result, banks are compelled to: Restructure branch networks; Invest heavily in digital infrastructure; Strengthening cybersecurity systems; Enhance artificial intelligence and data analytics capabilities.\n\nTherefore, banks, FinTech companies, and regulatory authorities must continuously strengthen security frameworks, compliance standards, and risk management systems to ensure financial stability and consumer protection.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, successful PayPal integration will require: Strong regulatory oversight; Effective cybersecurity measures; Collaboration among banks, policymakers, regulators, and FinTech firms; Compliance with international financial regulations.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s banking sector is gradually modernising through improved digital banking services, online payment gateways, mobile applications, and foreign currency services. Financial institutions increasingly recognise that digital transformation is essential for long-term competitiveness and sustainability.\n\nThe future of banking will likely involve deeper collaboration between traditional financial institutions and digital technology companies. Banks that successfully adapt to technological advancement and evolving customer expectations will remain resilient in the rapidly changing global financial environment.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s journey toward broader PayPal integration reflects the nation\u2019s commitment to participating actively in the global digital economy. PayPal has transformed the international financial landscape by introducing secure, efficient, and customer-friendly digital payment solutions.\n\nWhile the platform presents challenges for traditional commercial banks through increased competition and reduced fee-based income, it also encourages modernisation, innovation, operational efficiency, and enhanced customer service across the banking sector."
