    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond revenue: The power of the Taxpayer Charter\n\nIn every country, taxation is more than a legal requirement. It is a social contract. Citizens contribute a portion of their income to fund public services, and in return, the State has a responsibility to treat taxpayers fairly, transparently, and respectfully. A Taxpayer Charter is designed to make this relationship clear.\n\nAround the world, tax administrations have adopted Taxpayer Charters to define the rights and obligations of taxpayers. One of the earliest modern examples was introduced by the Inland Revenue in the United Kingdom in the 1980s. Since then, many administrations including the Australian Taxation Office and the Canada Revenue Agency have formalised charters to strengthen trust and improve voluntary compliance. International institutions such as the OECD have consistently emphasised the importance of clear taxpayer rights frameworks as part of modern tax governance.\n\nSri Lanka took a significant step in this direction when the Inland Revenue Department of Sri Lanka officially published its Taxpayer Charter in 2023 (available at www.ird.gov.lk \u2192 Publications \u2192 Taxpayer Charter). The introduction of the Charter marked an important milestone in strengthening transparency, service standards, and public confidence in the tax system.\n\nA Taxpayer Charter is not merely a policy document. It is a public commitment. It clearly defines how taxpayers will be treated and what is expected from them in return. By setting out both rights and obligations, the Charter creates a balanced and respectful framework for engagement.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Taxpayer Charter outlines nine fundamental rights of taxpayers. These include the right to:\n\nThese rights ensure that dignity and professionalism guide every interaction between the Department and the public.\n\nAt the same time, the Charter sets out four key obligations. Taxpayers are expected to:"
