    "md5": "5c19d4f58a606c6c83bdaa785ac5945c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "Throughout history, several prominent intellectuals and scholars have expressed admiration for Buddhist philosophy. The distinguished Pali scholar, Professor T.W. Rhys Davids, once observed that after studying many of the world\u2019s religions, he found none that surpassed the depth and beauty of the Buddha\u2019s teaching. Likewise, the renowned physicist Albert Einstein reportedly remarked that if there were a religion compatible with modern scientific needs, it would be Buddhism.\n\nAs Professor Arjuna de Soysa has pointed out, Buddhism is not scientific, nor is science Buddhistic. Attempts to force complete agreement between the two often result in superficial conclusions. Scientific theories evolve and change over time, while the Dhamma addresses existential realities connected to human experience and mental transformation\n\n.Nevertheless, Buddhism\u2019s openness to inquiry distinguishes it from rigid systems of belief. The Buddha never demanded unquestioning loyalty. He invited investigation. This spirit of examination is especially relevant in a modern world where individuals increasingly seek meaning beyond inherited tradition.\n\nQuestions surrounding the preservation and interpretation of religious teachings also deserve thoughtful reflection. Since the Buddha lived more than 2,500 years ago, some naturally ask how his teachings were preserved with complete accuracy over centuries of oral transmission before being written down. Such questions should not be dismissed as irreverent or hostile. In fact, the Buddha himself encouraged thoughtful inquiry rather than blind acceptance.\n\nHuman interpretation, memory, and cultural influence inevitably shape the transmission of all religious traditions. Yet the enduring strength of the Dhamma lies not merely in textual preservation, but in its practical applicability. Its value is ultimately tested through experience, ethical conduct, and mental cultivation."
  },
