    "md5": "184d36898990cf8c60b7367393fab4f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1722,
    "chunk_text": "Human interpretation, memory, and cultural influence inevitably shape the transmission of all religious traditions. Yet the enduring strength of the Dhamma lies not merely in textual preservation, but in its practical applicability. Its value is ultimately tested through experience, ethical conduct, and mental cultivation.\n\nThe Buddha recognized long ago that greed, hatred, and ignorance are the root causes of suffering. Technological progress alone cannot eliminate these destructive tendencies. Without moral wisdom, scientific advancement may simply increase humanity\u2019s capacity for harm.\n\nThis is why the Dhamma places such importance on compassion, mindfulness, and ethical responsibility. Buddhism encourages individuals to recognize the interconnections of life and cultivate concern for the well being of all living beings. Human beings are urged to move beyond narrow self-interest and develop qualities such as generosity, patience, non-violence, and loving-kindness.\n\nImportantly, the Dhamma does not reject reason or critical thought. At the same time, it recognizes that human understanding has limits. Even science itself often operates through probabilities, models, and evolving theories rather than absolute certainty. Many scientific concepts remain incomplete or debated despite extensive research.\n\nFor example, debates continue regarding consciousness, the origins of life, and the precise mechanisms of evolution and speciation. Scientific knowledge constantly evolves as new discoveries emerge. Buddhism similarly acknowledges the complexity and changing nature of existence, though it approaches these questions from the perspective of direct human experience rather than laboratory analysis."
