    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "Kalawana bus stand made gateway for Sinharaja conservation awareness\n\nThe main bus stand in Kalawana has been transformed into a public information hub dedicated to the conservation of the Sinharaja rainforest, aimed at educating both local communities and visiting tourists about the ecological significance of Sri Lanka\u2019s last remaining tropical rainforest.\n\nThe programme, which was officially opened on May 24, has been implemented by the Movement for Land and Agricultural Reform (MONLAR) in collaboration with local authorities, environmental groups and transport institutions.\n\nEnvironmental activist Sajeewa Chamikara said the project was designed to raise awareness among the thousands of commuters and travellers who pass through Kalawana on their way to the Sinharaja World Heritage Forest Reserve.\n\n\u201cKalawana is the principal town used by people living around Sinharaja as well as by local and foreign tourists visiting the rainforest. We wanted to transform the bus stand into a space where people could understand the biodiversity, environmental importance and conservation needs of Sinharaja,\u201d Chamikara said.\n\nHe noted that the initiative was also intended to promote environmentally sustainable agricultural practices among communities living around the rainforest ecosystem.\n\nThe project has been carried out under MONLAR\u2019s tropical rainforest conservation programme, which advocates ecological farming and agroforestry concepts to gradually convert monocrop tea lands into diversified multi-crop cultivation systems.\n\nAccording to Chamikara, protecting the Sinharaja ecosystem requires not only forest conservation measures but also greater awareness among surrounding communities regarding sustainable land use and ecological farming practices."
