    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "According to Chamikara, protecting the Sinharaja ecosystem requires not only forest conservation measures but also greater awareness among surrounding communities regarding sustainable land use and ecological farming practices.\n\nThe original proposal for the project was put forward by Aravinda Lakmal, terminal manager of the Kalawana Depot of the Sabaragamuwa Road Passenger Transport Authority; Rajitha Chathuranga Pitigala, chairman of the Kalawana Urban Development Foundation; and environmental advocate Upali Jayawardena of the Pitigalakanda and Mannawatta areas.\n\nThe initiative received support from the Kalawana Pradeshiya Sabha headed by Chairman Sumith Priyantha Somadasa, the Sabaragamuwa Road Passenger Transport Authority, the Kalawana Urban Development Foundation and the Kalawana Bus Friends Association.\n\nPhotographs used in the exhibition were contributed by MONLAR, reptile and amphibian researcher Udaya Chanaka, and researcher Shalith Karunaratne of the Ambagahakanda Sinharaja Friends Organisation. Artwork by Inoka Dilrukshi D. Herath, an art teacher at Rambukkana Sujatha Vidyalaya, was also used in the displays.\n\nMONLAR provided financial support for the preparation of exhibition content, graphic design, printing and installation work.\n\nChamikara called on schoolchildren, tourists and the general public visiting Sinharaja to stop at the Kalawana bus stand and view the displays.\n\n\u201cWe hope these exhibition boards will help people better understand why rainforests such as Sinharaja must be protected and encourage the public to actively contribute towards conservation efforts,\u201d he said.\n\nAuthenticity of early Buddhism and its relevance to the present\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
